Intuition
We need to find two non-overlapping subarrays whose sums are equal to target, and minimize their total length.
The key is to solve the problem from left to right.
For every position, keep track of the shortest valid subarray that ends before the current position.
When we find a new subarray with sum target, we can combine it with the best previous subarray.
Because the array contains positive numbers, we can use a sliding window to find every subarray with sum target.
At each right pointer:
Expand the window by adding nums[right].
If the sum becomes greater than target, move left until the sum is valid again.
When the sum equals target, we found a valid subarray.
Check the shortest valid subarray that appeared before this one.
Update the answer with their combined length.
Store the current subarray as the best subarray ending at or before this position.
The important part is maintaining:
best[i] = minimum length of a target-sum subarray completely inside indices 0...i
Then, if the current subarray is [left, right], any previous subarray ending before left is automatically non-overlapping.
So we use:
best[left - 1] + (right - left + 1)
to form the answer.
Pattern
Sliding Window + Prefix Minimum DP
Find current target-sum subarray
↓
Find shortest target-sum subarray before it
↓
Combine their lengths
↓
Take minimum

Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(n)
